{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The reason focuses on the outcomes of the lotteries and considers differences between them negligible if they are similar in magnitude. Here, Lottery A offers 2500€ (33%), 2400€ (66%), and 0€ (1%), while Lottery B offers 2400€ with 100% probability. The outcomes of both lotteries are close in value (2500€ vs. 2400€), so the difference may be considered negligible. The reason then prefers the lottery with the higher probability of a favorable outcome. A favorable outcome can be interpreted as receiving a non-zero, positive amount. For Lottery A, the probability of a favorable outcome (i.e., winning either 2500€ or 2400€) is 99% (33% + 66%). For Lottery B, the probability is 100%. Thus, Lottery B has a slightly higher probability of a favorable outcome and would be preferred by this reason.",
  "step_2": "The verbal report states: 'Sicherer Gewinn in vergleichbarer Höhe', which translates to 'A safer win of comparable magnitude'. This indicates that the individual considered the similarity in the amounts (comparable magnitude) and placed value on the certainty (safety) of the outcome. The individual appears to have focused on two aspects: (1) the outcomes of the two lotteries are similar (2400€ vs. 2500€), and (2) one option provides a sure outcome. The justification centers on preferring certainty when outcomes are similar, which aligns with favoring a higher probability of a favorable (i.e., non-zero) outcome when outcome differences are negligible.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason—namely, the similarity of outcomes and the comparison of probabilities of favorable outcomes—matches what the individual considered. The individual noted the comparable size of the outcomes (negligible difference) and emphasized the safety (certainty) of the win, which corresponds to a higher probability of a favorable outcome. The decision rule implied by the reason (prefer the lottery with higher probability of a favorable outcome when outcome differences are negligible) aligns with the individual’s stated justification. Although the individual does not explicitly mention probabilities or define 'favorable outcome', the emphasis on 'sicherer' (safer) and 'vergleichbarer Höhe' (comparable amount) closely mirrors the logic of the decision reason. Therefore, the verbal report is consistent with the use of this reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 95
}

{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The lotteries have distinct outcomes and probabilities. Lottery A offers 2400 Euros with 34% probability and 0 Euros with 66%. Lottery B offers 2500 Euros with 33% probability and 0 Euros with 67%. The outcomes (2400 vs 2500 Euros) differ by only 100 Euros, which may be considered negligible as per the reason's assumption. The reason focuses on the probability of a favorable outcome—here, any positive outcome (i.e., non-zero) is favorable. Lottery A has a 34% chance of a positive outcome, while Lottery B has a 33% chance. Thus, Lottery A has a higher probability of a favorable outcome and would be preferred by the reason.",
